{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00352 - MONDO_18256",
  "verbalization": "Tioguanine shows a strong association with Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) through a series of indications that link it to AML subtypes, including Inherited AML and Unclassified AML. These associations are supported by a consistent pattern of indication relationships, suggesting that Tioguanine may have a role in the pathogenesis of AML. The presence of a direct indication from Tioguanine to AML with the specific translocation T(8;16)(p11;p13) implies a potential mechanism of action that could target a biological pathway relevant to this subtype of AML. The evidence suggests that Tioguanine may influence AML progression through a mechanism that is not yet fully understood, but it is plausible that it interacts with cellular processes that are dysregulated in this disease. The integration of multiple indications across different AML subtypes supports the hypothesis that Tioguanine may have a broader role in AML pathogenesis, though further mechanistic studies are needed to confirm its specific mode of action.",
